/*
    ==================== PREFIX SUM – MASTER REVISION NOTES ====================

    Core Idea:
    -----------
    Prefix Sum helps us convert repeated range computations into O(1).

    prefix[i] = sum of elements from index 0 → i

    So,
    sum(l → r) = prefix[r] - prefix[l - 1]

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- 1. BASIC RANGE SUM QUERY ---------------------------
    Pattern:
    - Given array, answer multiple sum queries efficiently

    Use Case:
    - "Find sum between L and R many times"

    Template:
    ---------
    int[] prefix = new int[n];
    prefix[0] = arr[0];

    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        prefix[i] = prefix[i - 1] + arr[i];
    }

    // Query
    int sum = prefix[r] - (l > 0 ? prefix[l - 1] : 0);

    Time: O(n + q)

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- 2. COUNT OF SUBARRAYS WITH SUM = K -----------------
    Pattern:
    - Use prefix + hashmap

    Key Idea:
    - prefix[j] - prefix[i] = k
    ⇒ prefix[i] = prefix[j] - k

    Template:
    ---------
    Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
    map.put(0, 1);

    int sum = 0, count = 0;

    for (int num : nums) {
        sum += num;

        if (map.containsKey(sum - k)) {
            count += map.get(sum - k);
        }

        map.put(sum, map.getOrDefault(sum, 0) + 1);
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- 3. LONGEST SUBARRAY WITH SUM = K -------------------
    Pattern:
    - Store first occurrence of prefix sum

    Template:
    ---------
    Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
    map.put(0, -1);

    int sum = 0, maxLen = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        sum += nums[i];

        if (map.containsKey(sum - k)) {
            maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, i - map.get(sum - k));
        }

        map.putIfAbsent(sum, i);
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- 4. COUNT SUBARRAYS WITH SUM ≤ K --------------------
    Pattern:
    - Prefix sum + sliding window (if all positive)

    Template:
    ---------
    int left = 0, sum = 0, count = 0;

    for (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {
        sum += nums[right];

        while (sum > k) {
            sum -= nums[left++];
        }

        count += (right - left + 1);
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- 5. PREFIX SUM + MOD (DIVISIBLE BY K) ---------------
    Pattern:
    - (prefix % k) matching

    Template:
    ---------
    Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
    map.put(0, 1);

    int sum = 0, count = 0;

    for (int num : nums) {
        sum += num;
        int mod = ((sum % k) + k) % k;

        count += map.getOrDefault(mod, 0);
        map.put(mod, map.getOrDefault(mod, 0) + 1);
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- 6. RANGE UPDATE (DIFFERENCE ARRAY) -----------------
    Pattern:
    - Multiple range updates efficiently

    Idea:
    - Instead of updating entire range, mark boundaries

    Template:
    ---------
    int[] diff = new int[n];

    // Update [l, r] by val
    diff[l] += val;
    if (r + 1 < n) diff[r + 1] -= val;

    // Build final array
    arr[0] = diff[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        arr[i] = arr[i - 1] + diff[i];
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- 7. 2D PREFIX SUM (MATRIX) --------------------------
    Pattern:
    - Submatrix sum queries

    Formula:
    ---------
    prefix[i][j] = matrix[i][j]
                + prefix[i-1][j]
                + prefix[i][j-1]
                - prefix[i-1][j-1]

    Query:
    ---------
    sum = prefix[r2][c2]
        - prefix[r1-1][c2]
        - prefix[r2][c1-1]
        + prefix[r1-1][c1-1]

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- 8. PREFIX MIN / MAX -------------------------------
    Pattern:
    - Track best till index

    Template:
    ---------
    prefixMin[0] = arr[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        prefixMin[i] = Math.min(prefixMin[i - 1], arr[i]);
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- 9. SPLIT ARRAY / PARTITION -------------------------
    Pattern:
    - Split into left + right using prefix

    Template:
    ---------
    int total = prefix[n - 1];

    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        int left = prefix[i];
        int right = total - prefix[i];

        if (condition) count++;
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- 10. PREFIX SUM ON STRINGS --------------------------
    Pattern:
    - Count frequency till index

    Example:
    ---------
    int[][] freq = new int[n][26];

    freq[0][s.charAt(0) - 'a']++;

    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        freq[i] = freq[i - 1].clone();
        freq[i][s.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- 11. PREFIX + LAST OCCURRENCE -----------------------
    Pattern:
    - Like Garbage Collection problem

    Idea:
    - Find last index of each type
    - Add prefix cost till that index

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- COMMON MISTAKES -----------------------------------
    1. Forgetting (l > 0 ? prefix[l-1] : 0)
    2. Negative modulo issues
    3. Using int instead of long (overflow)
    4. Off-by-one errors in indexing
    5. Misunderstanding prefix[i] meaning

    ==========================================================================

    ---------------------- GOLDEN RULE ---------------------------------------
    Whenever you see:
    - "range sum"
    - "subarray sum"
    - "multiple queries"
    - "cumulative computation"

    👉 Think PREFIX SUM first.

    ==========================================================================

    */

    /*
    ================== PREFIX SUM ON 0s and 1s (VERY IMPORTANT) ==================

    These problems look different but follow ONE CORE TRICK:

    👉 Convert 0 → -1
    👉 Keep 1 → +1

    Why?
    - Because equal 0s and 1s means:
        count(1) == count(0)
    ⇒ sum = 0

    ==========================================================================

    ------------------ 1. LONGEST SUBARRAY WITH EQUAL 0s & 1s ------------------
    Pattern:
    - Replace 0 with -1
    - Find longest subarray with sum = 0

    Template:
    ---------
    Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
    map.put(0, -1);

    int sum = 0, maxLen = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        sum += (nums[i] == 0 ? -1 : 1);

        if (map.containsKey(sum)) {
            maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, i - map.get(sum));
        } else {
            map.put(sum, i);
        }
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ------------------ 2. COUNT SUBARRAYS WITH EQUAL 0s & 1s -------------------
    Pattern:
    - Same conversion (0 → -1)
    - Count subarrays with sum = 0

    Template:
    ---------
    Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
    map.put(0, 1);

    int sum = 0, count = 0;

    for (int num : nums) {
        sum += (num == 0 ? -1 : 1);

        count += map.getOrDefault(sum, 0);
        map.put(sum, map.getOrDefault(sum, 0) + 1);
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ------------------ 3. LONGEST SUBARRAY WITH MORE 1s THAN 0s ----------------
    Pattern:
    - Convert 0 → -1
    - Find longest subarray with sum > 0

    Trick:
    - Use prefix + monotonic stack OR brute with prefix

    (Slightly advanced, less common in interviews)

    ==========================================================================

    ------------------ 4. COUNT SUBARRAYS WITH MORE 1s THAN 0s -----------------
    Pattern:
    - Convert 0 → -1
    - Count subarrays with sum > 0

    Approach:
    - Prefix sum + Fenwick Tree / TreeMap
    - Advanced (seen in hard problems)

    ==========================================================================

    ------------------ 5. FIXED SIZE WINDOW (SLIDING WINDOW) ------------------
    Pattern:
    - If window size is fixed → no prefix needed

    Example:
    ---------
    int count = 0, ones = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
        ones += nums[i];
    }

    count = ones;

    for (int i = k; i < n; i++) {
        ones += nums[i] - nums[i - k];
        count = Math.max(count, ones);
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ------------------ 6. PREFIX DIFFERENCE (COUNT 1s - COUNT 0s) --------------
    Pattern:
    - Directly track difference instead of modifying array

    Template:
    ---------
    int sum = 0;

    for (int num : nums) {
        if (num == 1) sum++;
        else sum--;
    }

    ==========================================================================

    ------------------ COMMON INTUITION ---------------------------------------
    1. Equal 0s and 1s → sum = 0
    2. More 1s → sum > 0
    3. More 0s → sum < 0

    👉 Everything reduces to prefix sum comparison

    ==========================================================================

    ------------------ COMMON MISTAKES ----------------------------------------
    1. Forgetting to convert 0 → -1
    2. Not initializing map with (0, -1) or (0, 1)
    3. Using sliding window when negative values exist ❌
    4. Confusing count vs length problems

    ==========================================================================

    ------------------ INTERVIEW GOLD -----------------------------------------
    Whenever you see:
    - "equal 0s and 1s"
    - "binary array balance"
    - "same number of 0 and 1"

    👉 Immediately think:
    ✔ Convert 0 → -1
    ✔ Use prefix sum + hashmap

*/